# Wefts

`Weft` defines the line-level AST nodes. Each weft is one source line, typed by the mode it sits in and the tokens found on it. Wefts are health-bearing `loomNode`s, so they carry `type`, `position`, and `health` like any node. A weft's position spans exactly one line. `LoomWeft`, the union of them all, is the element the tokeniser streams between passes.

## The heading weft

A `HeadingWeft` is a `#`-heading line. Its `headingStart` records the level for the reader, and `title` is the optional trimmed text after the marker — the title names the section, normalised to an identifier. The `specifier` and the `sink` are optional too: the specifier is the language label `{Lang}`, the sink is the bracketed file `[file]`, and a heading may carry either, both, or neither. The classifier emits the weft with incomplete placeholders for the subtokens it has not recognised, and the tokeniser settles them.

=>

export const HeadingWeftSchema = loomNode('HeadingWeft', {
  headingStart: HeadingStartTokenSchema,
  title: Schema.optional(HeadingTitleTokenSchema),
  specifier: Schema.optional(SpecifierTokenSchema),
  sink: Schema.optional(SinkTokenSchema),
})
export type HeadingWeft = typeof HeadingWeftSchema.Type

## The body wefts

The body wefts are the lines inside a section. An `ArrowWeft` is the `=>` line that opens code, carrying any inline `code` and the anchors on it. A `CodeWeft` is a later line in code mode — opaque to Loom, but its anchors are recognised. A `TildeWeft` is the `~` line that opens prose, carrying any inline `prose` and its anchors. A `ProseWeft` is a later line in prose mode — opaque too, but its anchors are recognised. A `PreambleWeft` is a default-mode line before any transition, carrying the warp declarations and any anchors on it.

Every body weft carries its `anchors`, because an anchor is read in code and in prose alike. A code anchor composes the named section; a prose anchor places a chapter or merely links to it. A later pass reads which plane an anchor takes from the weft it sits on.

A box sits in prose, so the line of colons that opens or closes one is a preamble weft or a prose weft. That weft carries the line's `Box` token in `box`, and holds no warp or anchor, since the line is all box. The lines of the box's body are ordinary preamble or prose wefts with no `box` of their own. A pass that wants a box's body takes the wefts between the line that opens it and the line that closes it, much as fenced code runs between its two fence wefts.

=>

export const ArrowWeftSchema = loomNode('ArrowWeft', {
  arrow: ArrowTokenSchema,
  code: Schema.optional(CodeTokenSchema),
  anchors: Schema.Array(WarpAnchorTokenSchema),
})
export type ArrowWeft = typeof ArrowWeftSchema.Type

export const TildeWeftSchema = loomNode('TildeWeft', {
  tilde: TildeTokenSchema,
  prose: Schema.optional(ProseTokenSchema),
  anchors: Schema.Array(WarpAnchorTokenSchema),
})
export type TildeWeft = typeof TildeWeftSchema.Type

export const PreambleWeftSchema = loomNode('PreambleWeft', {
  warps: Schema.Array(WarpTokenSchema),
  anchors: Schema.Array(WarpAnchorTokenSchema),
  box: Schema.optional(BoxTokenSchema),
})
export type PreambleWeft = typeof PreambleWeftSchema.Type

export const ProseWeftSchema = loomNode('ProseWeft', {
  anchors: Schema.Array(WarpAnchorTokenSchema),
  box: Schema.optional(BoxTokenSchema),
})
export type ProseWeft = typeof ProseWeftSchema.Type

export const CodeWeftSchema = loomNode('CodeWeft', {
  anchors: Schema.Array(WarpAnchorTokenSchema),
})
export type CodeWeft = typeof CodeWeftSchema.Type

~

Fenced code adds one more. A `FenceWeft` is a fence line that bounds fenced code, either the one that opens it straight after its arrow or the one that closes it. Its `side` says which, so no later pass has to pair the fences up again. Its `fence` is the run of backticks, and an opening fence may carry a `label`, the language name written after them. A fence weft is neither code nor prose: the code between the two fences is code wefts, and the fence lines themselves compose into nothing. A fence line holds no anchor, so its `anchors` stay empty, and a pass that gathers a section's anchors can still ask every body weft for them. A fence in prose is not a fence weft. It opens a quote, and its lines stay prose wefts.

=>

export const FenceWeftSchema = loomNode('FenceWeft', {
  side: Schema.Literals(['open', 'close']),
  fence: CodeFenceTokenSchema,
  label: Schema.optional(FenceLabelTokenSchema),
  anchors: Schema.Array(WarpAnchorTokenSchema),
})
export type FenceWeft = typeof FenceWeftSchema.Type

## The frontmatter weft

A `FrontmatterWeft` is one line of the frontmatter block: a fence, a line that names the loom's place, or a `key: value` field. One weft kind covers them all, and each line fills the slots it owns. A fence line fills `fence`. A `Part` line fills `part` and `partName`, a `Chapter` line fills `chapter` and `title`, and the joined `Part …, Chapter …` line fills `part`, `chapter`, and `title`. A `Discussion` line, alone or joined to its part, fills `discussion` in place of `chapter`. A trope chapter's line fills `trope` beside its chapter and title, a `Rooted in` line fills `rootedIn`, and an `Entries` line fills `entries`, one token for each chapter it names. A `Book` line fills `book`. A `Package` or `Language` line fills `key` and `value`.

One optional slot for each part of a line mirrors the heading weft, which carries its title, specifier, and sink the same way. The document builder gathers a file's frontmatter wefts into one `LoomFrontmatter`.

=>

export const FrontmatterWeftSchema = loomNode('FrontmatterWeft', {
  fence: Schema.optional(FrontmatterFenceTokenSchema),
  part: Schema.optional(FrontmatterPartTokenSchema),
  partName: Schema.optional(FrontmatterPartNameTokenSchema),
  chapter: Schema.optional(FrontmatterChapterTokenSchema),
  discussion: Schema.optional(FrontmatterDiscussionTokenSchema),
  trope: Schema.optional(FrontmatterTropeTokenSchema),
  rootedIn: Schema.optional(FrontmatterTropeTokenSchema),
  entries: Schema.optional(Schema.Array(FrontmatterEntryTokenSchema)),
  book: Schema.optional(FrontmatterBookTokenSchema),
  title: Schema.optional(FrontmatterTitleTokenSchema),
  key: Schema.optional(FrontmatterKeyTokenSchema),
  value: Schema.optional(FrontmatterValueTokenSchema),
})
export type FrontmatterWeft = typeof FrontmatterWeftSchema.Type

## The divider weft

A `DividerWeft` is a line of dashes that divides a chapter, holding its `dashes`. A divider ends the section above it and opens a division, as a heading opens a section. The weft carries nothing from the line above it, because a weft spans one line. The document builder pairs a divider with its label, the line of prose directly above it, as the chapter "The document builder" describes.

=>

export const DividerWeftSchema = loomNode('DividerWeft', {
  dashes: DividerTokenSchema,
})
export type DividerWeft = typeof DividerWeftSchema.Type

## The weft unions

`LoomWeft` is the union of every weft kind — the tokeniser's stream element. `SectionBodyWeft` is the narrower union the grammar admits inside a section body: arrow, code, fence, tilde, and prose, in the forward-only order the classifier enforces.

=>

export const LoomWeftSchema = Schema.Union([
  HeadingWeftSchema,
  ArrowWeftSchema,
  TildeWeftSchema,
  PreambleWeftSchema,
  ProseWeftSchema,
  CodeWeftSchema,
  FenceWeftSchema,
  FrontmatterWeftSchema,
  DividerWeftSchema,
])
export type LoomWeft = typeof LoomWeftSchema.Type

export const SectionBodyWeftSchema = Schema.Union([
  ArrowWeftSchema,
  CodeWeftSchema,
  FenceWeftSchema,
  TildeWeftSchema,
  ProseWeftSchema,
])
export type SectionBodyWeft = typeof SectionBodyWeftSchema.Type

~

`boxOf` reads the `Box` token a weft carries. Only a preamble weft or a prose weft can carry one, so every other weft has none.

=>

export const boxOf = (weft: LoomWeft): Option.Option<BoxToken> =>
  Match.value(weft).pipe(
    Match.when({ type: 'PreambleWeft' }, (line) => Option.fromNullishOr(line.box)),
    Match.when({ type: 'ProseWeft' }, (line) => Option.fromNullishOr(line.box)),
    Match.orElse(() => Option.none<BoxToken>()),
  )

~

A box spans many lines, so a pass that reads wefts one at a time keeps a flag that says whether a box is open, and `nextBox` moves the flag past one weft. A line that opens a box sets it, a line that closes the box clears it, and a stray leaves it as it was, since a stray opens and closes nothing. So does a weft that carries no box. The classifier keeps the flag to mark the lines that close a box, and the tokeniser keeps it because no quote opens inside a box.

=>

export const nextBox = (inBox: boolean, weft: LoomWeft): boolean =>
  Option.match(boxOf(weft), {
    onNone: () => inBox,
    onSome: (box) =>
      Match.value(box.side).pipe(
        Match.when('open', () => true),
        Match.when('close', () => false),
        Match.when('stray', () => inBox),
        Match.exhaustive,
      ),
  })

## What this module draws on

Each weft is a `loomNode` built from Effect's `Schema`; its subtokens are the heading, arrow, tilde, code, prose, fence, box, divider, specifier, sink, warp, and anchor schemas from `LoomTokens`. `Match` and `Option` read the box a weft carries and follow whether a box is open.

=>

import { Match, Option, Schema } from 'effect'
import { loomNode } from '#ast/LoomNode'
import {
  ArrowTokenSchema,
  type BoxToken,
  BoxTokenSchema,
  CodeFenceTokenSchema,
  CodeTokenSchema,
  DividerTokenSchema,
  FenceLabelTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterBookTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterChapterTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterDiscussionTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterTropeTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterEntryTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterFenceTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterKeyTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterPartTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterPartNameTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterTitleTokenSchema,
  FrontmatterValueTokenSchema,
  HeadingStartTokenSchema,
  HeadingTitleTokenSchema,
  ProseTokenSchema,
  SinkTokenSchema,
  SpecifierTokenSchema,
  TildeTokenSchema,
  WarpAnchorTokenSchema,
  WarpTokenSchema,
} from './LoomTokens'

